sec.p:
This biophysical interaction and ion-exchange mechanism across the membrane of a neuronal cell can be described as a Hodgkin–Huxley type dynamical process, represented by the following dynamical system as described in [78].
(1)
dVdt=−1Cm(ICl+INa+IK+Ipump)dndt=n∞(V)−nτndΔ[K+]intdt=−γωi(IK−2Ipump)d[K+]gdt=ϵ([K+]bath−[K+]ext) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{align*} \frac{\mathrm{d} V}{\mathrm{~d} t} & =-\frac{1}{C_{m}}\left(I_{C l}+I_{N a}+I_{K}+I_{\text {pump }}\right) \\ \frac{\mathrm{d} n}{\mathrm{~d} t} & =\frac{n_{\infty}(V)-n}{\tau_{n}} \\ \frac{\mathrm{~d} \Delta\left[K^{+}\right]_{i n t}}{\mathrm{~d} t} & =-\frac{\gamma}{\omega_{i}}\left(I_{K}-2 I_{\text {pump }}\right) \tag{1}\\ \frac{\mathrm{d}\left[K^{+}\right]_{g}}{\mathrm{~d} t} & =\epsilon\left(\left[K^{+}\right]_{\text {bath }}-\left[K^{+}\right]_{\text {ext }}\right) \end{align*}$$\end{document}
This model represents the ion-exchange mechanism of a single conductance-based neuron in terms of membrane potential V, the potassium conductance gating variable n, intracellular potassium concentration variation Δ[K+]int, and extracellular potassium buffering by the external bath [K+]g. This mechanism considers ion exchange through the chloride, sodium, potassium, voltage-gated channels, intracellular sodium, and extracellular potassium concentration gradients and leak currents. The intrinsic ionic currents, the sodium-potassium pump current, and potassium diffusion regulate the different ion concentrations. The Nernst equation was used to couple the neuron’s membrane potential with the concentrations of the ionic currents. This mechanism gives rise to a slow-fast dynamical system in which the membrane potential V and potassium conductance gating variable n constitute the fast subsystem and the slow subsystem is represented in terms of the variation in the intracellular potassium concentration Δ[K+]int and extracellular potassium buffering by the external bath [K+]g (Fig. 2.b). In Eq. (1) the input currents due to different ionic substances and pump are represented as follows [68, 78]:
(2)
INa=(gNa,l+gNam∞(V)h(n))(V−26.64log⁡([Na+]ext[Na+]int)),IK=(gK,l+gKn)(V−26.64log⁡([K+]ext[K+]int)),ICl=gCl(V+26.64log⁡([Cl−]ext[Cl−]int)),Ipump=ρ11+exp⁡(10.5−0.5[Na+]int)11+exp⁡(5.5−[K+]ext). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{align*} I_{N a} & =\left(g_{N a, l}+g_{N a} m_{\infty}(V) h(n)\right)\left(V-26.64 \log \left(\frac{\left[N a^{+}\right]_{e x t}}{\left[N a^{+}\right]_{i n t}}\right)\right), \\ I_{K} & =\left(g_{K, l}+g_{K} n\right)\left(V-26.64 \log \left(\frac{\left[K^{+}\right]_{e x t}}{\left[K^{+}\right]_{i n t}}\right)\right), \tag{2}\\ I_{C l} & =g_{C l}\left(V+26.64 \log \left(\frac{\left[C l^{-}\right]_{e x t}}{\left[C l^{-}\right]_{i n t}}\right)\right), \\ I_{p u m p} & =\rho \frac{1}{1+\exp \left(10.5-0.5\left[N a^{+}\right]_{i n t}\right)} \frac{1}{1+\exp \left(5.5-\left[K^{+}\right]_{e x t}\right)} . \end{align*}$$\end{document}
The gating functions for the conductance are modeled as
(3)
n∞(V)=11+exp⁡(−30−V5),m∞(V)=11+exp⁡(−22−V15),h(n)=1.1−11+exp⁡(−8(n−0.4)). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{align*} n_{\infty}(V) & =\frac{1}{1+\exp \left(\frac{-30-V}{5}\right)}, \\ m_{\infty}(V) & =\frac{1}{1+\exp \left(\frac{-22-V}{15}\right)}, \tag{3}\\ h(n) & =1.1-\frac{1}{1+\exp (-8(n-0.4))} . \end{align*}$$\end{document}
Notice that, compared to [78], these equations were reparametrized to account for changes in timescales (see also 1). In this model the concentration of chloride ions inside and outside of the membrane is invariant. i.e., [Cl−]ext = [Cl−]0,ext and [Cl−]int = [Cl−]0,int. The extracellular and intracellular concentrations of sodium and potassium ions are represented in terms of the state variables Δ[K+]int, [K+]g as follows [78]:
(4)
Δ[Na+]int=−Δ[K+]intΔ[Na+]ext=−βΔ[Na+]intΔ[K+]ext=−βΔ[K+]int[K+]int=[K+]0,int+Δ[K+]int[Na]int=[Na+]0,int+Δ[Na+]int[Na+]ext=[Na+]0,ext+Δ[Na+]ext[K+]ext=[K+]0,ext+Δ[K+]ext+[K+]g \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{align*} \Delta\left[N a^{+}\right]_{i n t} & =-\Delta\left[K^{+}\right]_{i n t} \\ \Delta\left[N a^{+}\right]_{e x t} & =-\beta \Delta\left[N a^{+}\right]_{i n t} \\ \Delta\left[K^{+}\right]_{e x t} & =-\beta \Delta\left[K^{+}\right]_{i n t} \\ {\left[K^{+}\right]_{i n t} } & =\left[K^{+}\right]_{0, i n t}+\Delta\left[K^{+}\right]_{i n t} \tag{4}\\ {[N a]_{i n t} } & =\left[N a^{+}\right]_{0, i n t}+\Delta\left[N a^{+}\right]_{i n t} \\ {\left[N a^{+}\right]_{e x t} } & =\left[N a^{+}\right]_{0, e x t}+\Delta\left[N a^{+}\right]_{e x t} \\ {\left[K^{+}\right]_{e x t} } & =\left[K^{+}\right]_{0, e x t}+\Delta\left[K^{+}\right]_{e x t}+\left[K^{+}\right]_{g} \end{align*}$$\end{document}
The biophysically relevant values of the parameters could be obtained from several previous studies, both from in vivo and in vitro experiments [68]. The parameters used for the single-neuron simulations are shown in Table-1, and allow the reproduction of several bursting and oscillatory regimes by varying the parameter [K+]bath (Fig. 2.a).

We aim to develop a mean-field model for a heterogeneous population of the all-to-all coupled biophysical neurons described by Eq. (1). Our strategy consists of using a series of approximations and heuristic arguments to obtain a putative functional form for the mean-field model, which is capable of simulating the mean activity of a large population of Hodgkin–Huxley (HH) type neurons operating near synchrony. The basic mechanism of synaptic transmission can be described by the arrival of a spike at the presynaptic terminal, resulting in an influx of calcium which depolarizes the presynaptic terminals and thus stimulates the release of neurotransmitters into the synaptic cleft. The neurotransmitters play a crucial role in opening the postsynaptic channels, which causes the flow of ionic currents across the membrane. This flow creates an (inhibitory or excitatory) post-synaptic potential in the efferent neuron. We take this into account by adding a synaptic input current in the membrane potential equation at neuron i using a conductance-based model
(5)
Isyn,i=gsyn,i(t)(Esyn−Vi), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} I_{\text {syn }, i}=g_{\text {syn }, i}(t)\left(E_{\text {syn }}-V_{i}\right), \tag{5} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where gsyn,i is the synaptic conductance, Vi is the postsynaptic potential and Esyn is the synaptic reversal potential at which the direction of the net current flow reverses. In the present work, we fixed Esyn = 0mV, corresponding to excitatory neurons. Lower reversal potential values (e.g., Esyn = −80mV) model inhibitory neurons. Typically, at the arrival time tjk\begin{document}$t_{j}^{k}$\end{document} of the k-th spike from neuron j, the synaptic conductance quickly rises and consequently undergoes an exponential decay with some rate constant. This mechanism is captured by defining the conductance dynamics gsyn,i ∝ Jsi(t) where J is a constant conductance value and the adimensional dynamical variable si(t) is the solution of the following equation (see for example [28]):
(6)
Q^si(t)=ssynN∑j=1N∑kWijδ(t−tjk), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \hat{Q} s_{i}(t)=\frac{s_{\text {syn }}}{N} \sum_{j=1}^{N} \sum_{k} W_{i j} \delta\left(t-t_{j}^{k}\right), \tag{6} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where ssyn = 1 is the synaptic strength, Wij is the synaptic weight which here we assume to be equal to 1 for all neurons i and j, and the operator Q^\begin{document}$\hat{Q}$\end{document} can be defined with various levels of accuracy as
(7)
Q^=1,Q^=(1+τaddt),Q^=(1+τaddt)(1+τbddt),… \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \hat{Q}=\mathbb{1}, \hat{Q}=\left(1+\tau_{a} \frac{\mathrm{~d}}{\mathrm{~d} t}\right), \hat{Q}=\left(1+\tau_{a} \frac{\mathrm{~d}}{\mathrm{~d} t}\right)\left(1+\tau_{b} \frac{\mathrm{~d}}{\mathrm{~d} t}\right), \ldots \tag{7} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
Here, for simplicity, we select the first model, which leads to
(8)
si=1N∑j=1N∑kδ(t−tjk). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} s_{i}=\frac{1}{N} \sum_{j=1}^{N} \sum_{k} \delta\left(t-t_{j}^{k}\right) . \tag{8} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
Under the assumption of instantaneous synaptic transmission and homogeneous all-to-all coupling, the synaptic activation variable si is the same for all neurons and corresponds to the population firing rate, which we denote by r.

In the coupled system, the membrane potential equation for neuron i is defined by:

(9)

CmdVidt=−(ICl,i+INa,i+IK,i+Ipump,i)+Jsi(Esyn−Vi)+ηi
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} C_{m} \frac{d V_{i}}{d t}=-\left(I_{C l, i}+I_{N a, i}+I_{K, i}+I_{p u m p, i}\right)+J s_{i}\left(E_{s y n}-V_{i}\right)+\eta_{i} \tag{9} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}



where we fix the capacitance to Cm = 1nF, and the term ηi represents a heterogeneous noise current distributed according to a Lorentzian distribution with half-width Δ and location of the center at η¯\begin{document}$\bar{\eta}$\end{document}

(10)

g(η)=1πΔ(η−η¯)2+Δ2.
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} g(\eta)=\frac{1}{\pi} \frac{\Delta}{(\eta-\bar{\eta})^{2}+\Delta^{2}} . \tag{10} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

Therefore, we write the population density function for the fast subsystem as
(12)
ρ(t,V,n,η)=ρn(t,n∣V,η)ρV(t,V∣η)g(η) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \rho(t, V, n, \eta)=\rho^{n}(t, n \mid V, \eta) \rho^{V}(t, V \mid \eta) g(\eta) \tag{12} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
which was here expressed in the conditional form independent of the potassium variables. The continuity equation reads
(13)
∂∂tρ(t,V,n,η)+∇⋅J(t,V,n,s,η)=0 \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \frac{\partial}{\partial t} \rho(t, V, n, \eta)+\nabla \cdot \mathcal{J}(t, V, n, s, \eta)=0 \tag{13} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where the flux in the V and n directions is
(14)
J(t,V,n,s,η)=(JV(t,V,n,s,η)Jn(t,V,n))=(GV(V,n,s,η)Gn(V,n))ρ(t,V,n,η), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{align*} \mathcal{J}(t, V, n, s, \eta) & =\binom{\mathcal{J}^{V}(t, V, n, s, \eta)}{\mathcal{J}^{n}(t, V, n)} \tag{14}\\ & =\binom{G^{V}(V, n, s, \eta)}{G^{n}(V, n)} \rho(t, V, n, \eta), \end{align*}$$\end{document}
With
(15)
GV(V,n,s,η)=V˙0+Isyn+η,Gn(V,n)=n∞(V)−nτn, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{align*} G^{V}(V, n, s, \eta) & =\dot{V}_{0}+I_{s y n}+\eta, \\ G^{n}(V, n) & =\frac{n_{\infty}(V)-n}{\tau_{n}}, \tag{15} \end{align*}$$\end{document}
And
(16)
V˙0=−1Cm(ICl+INa+IK+Ipump),Isyn=JCms(Esyn−V). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{align*} \dot{V}_{0} & =-\frac{1}{C_{m}}\left(I_{C l}+I_{N a}+I_{K}+I_{p u m p}\right), \\ I_{s y n} & =\frac{J}{C_{m}} s\left(E_{s y n}-V\right) . \tag{16} \end{align*}$$\end{document}
We note that, under the assumption of globally shared gating and ion concentration variables across the neuronal population, the resulting mean-field equations can also be derived using simpler methods as proposed by Guerriero et al. [58]. In this work, we follow the more general formalism of Chen and Campbell [59], which makes the role of key approximations (e.g., moment closure, vanishing flux at boundaries) explicit. This also facilitates potential generalizations to settings with partial heterogeneity or dynamic gating distributions.

Following [59], we obtain a modified version of the continuity equation by integrating the continuity equation 13 with respect to n. Using the normalization condition on the marginal density of n, the first term gives
(17)
∫∂n∂∂tρ(t,V,n,η)dn=∂∂t(ρV(t,V∣η)∫∂nρn(t,n∣V,η)dn)g(η)=∂∂tρV(t,V∣η)g(η). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{align*} \int_{\partial n} \frac{\partial}{\partial t} \rho(t, V, n, \eta) \mathrm{d} n & =\frac{\partial}{\partial t}\left(\rho^{V}(t, V \mid \eta) \int_{\partial n} \rho^{n}(t, n \mid V, \eta) \mathrm{d} n\right) g(\eta) \tag{17}\\ & =\frac{\partial}{\partial t} \rho^{V}(t, V \mid \eta) g(\eta) . \end{align*}$$\end{document}
Similarly, integrating the second term in the continuity equation 13 with respect to n we obtain
(18)
∫∂n∇⋅J(t,V,n,s,η)dn=∂∂V[GV(V,⟨n∣η⟩,s,η)ρV(t,V∣η)]g(η)+∫∂n∂∂n[Gn(V,n)ρn(t,n∣V,η)]dnρV(t,V∣η)g(η)=∂∂V[GV(V,⟨n∣η⟩,s,η)ρV(t,V∣η)]g(η)+[Gn(V,n)ρn(t,n∣V,η)]n=+∞ρV(t,V∣η)g(η)−[Gn(V,n)ρn(t,n∣V,η)]n=−∞ρV(t,V∣η)g(η)=∂∂V[GV(V,⟨n∣η⟩,s,η)ρV(t,V∣η)]g(η), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{align*} \int_{\partial n} \nabla \cdot \mathcal{J}(t, V, n, s, \eta) \mathrm{d} n= & \frac{\partial}{\partial V}\left[G^{V}(V,\langle n \mid \eta\rangle, s, \eta) \rho^{V}(t, V \mid \eta)\right] g(\eta) \\ & +\int_{\partial n} \frac{\partial}{\partial n}\left[G^{n}(V, n) \rho^{n}(t, n \mid V, \eta)\right] \mathrm{d} n \rho^{V}(t, V \mid \eta) g(\eta) \\ = & \frac{\partial}{\partial V}\left[G^{V}(V,\langle n \mid \eta\rangle, s, \eta) \rho^{V}(t, V \mid \eta)\right] g(\eta) \\ & +\left[G^{n}(V, n) \rho^{n}(t, n \mid V, \eta)\right]_{n=+\infty} \rho^{V}(t, V \mid \eta) g(\eta) \\ & -\left[G^{n}(V, n) \rho^{n}(t, n \mid V, \eta)\right]_{n=-\infty} \rho^{V}(t, V \mid \eta) g(\eta) \\ = & \frac{\partial}{\partial V}\left[G^{V}(V,\langle n \mid \eta\rangle, s, \eta) \rho^{V}(t, V \mid \eta)\right] g(\eta), \tag{18} \end{align*}$$\end{document}
where we assumed that the flux along n vanishes on the boundary ∂n. Next we assume a first-order moment closure condition for the variable n [59], justified by the numerical simulations of the full network (see Fig. S2) which show that for most of the neurons (close to 99 % for the value of Δ same as in the other simulations) the mean of the population is well capturing the behavior of the single neurons [122]. Finally, putting together these factors and assuming that n can be treated as a collective variable for each neuron (see Limitations of the model section) we arrive to
(19)
⟨n∣V,η⟩≃⟨n∣η⟩. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \langle n \mid V, \eta\rangle \simeq\langle n \mid \eta\rangle . \tag{19} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
From here we obtain a modified version of the continuity equation for the fast subsystem
(20)
∂∂tρV(t,V∣η)=−∂∂V[GV(V,⟨n∣η⟩,s,η)ρV(t,V∣η)]. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \frac{\partial}{\partial t} \rho^{V}(t, V \mid \eta)=-\frac{\partial}{\partial V}\left[G^{V}(V,\langle n \mid \eta\rangle, s, \eta) \rho^{V}(t, V \mid \eta)\right] . \tag{20} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
The validity of the first moment closure, Eqs. (19), as in [59], is supported by the numerical simulations, which show that, both, during the silent regime and when seizure-like events occur, ni for most neurons track the network averaged ⟨n |V, η⟩ . In particular, it is less than 2 % of the neurons that fire while the mean is low, and vice-versa, Fig. S2. In less synchronized scenarios (larger Δ or smaller J), however, this value would increase, but the mean would always capture the qualitative behaviour of the population.

Previous mean-field derivations have adopted neuron models described by discontinuous quadratic equations, where the neuron is firing at a threshold Vth, after which the voltage is reset to Vreset. In these models, the threshold and reset are taken in the limit Vth = ™Vreset → ∞, and the quadratic expression for the membrane potential dynamics guarantees that a neuron takes a finite time to reach the threshold [25]. Therefore, the firing rate can be defined as the flux at infinity i.e.,
(24)
r(t,η)=limVth→+∞ρV(t,Vth∣η)GV(Vth,⟨n∣η⟩,s,η) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} r(t, \eta)=\lim _{V_{t h} \rightarrow+\infty} \rho^{V}\left(t, V_{t h} \mid \eta\right) G^{V}\left(V_{t h},\langle n \mid \eta\rangle, s, \eta\right) \tag{24} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
In our continuous neuron model, the spiking and resetting are built into the dynamic equation and can be driven by several mechanisms, including slow potassium dynamics. Let us consider the case of a steady input current affecting the membrane potential, such that the nullcline function (here described by the piece-wise quadratic function) will have either one zero (stable fixed point) or three zeros (two stable and one unstable). In this case, we can describe the firing at the pitchfork bifurcation, where the system transitions from three fixed points to one. At the bifurcation point, a neuron with membrane potential values at −∞ will be first driven by the positive quadratic trend, until it crosses the V ⋆ value, after which the negative parabolic trend will bring the neuron onto the fixed point. To have an operational definition of the firing rate and simplify our problem, we consider that once the V ⋆ value is crossed the neuron membrane potential will reach the peak value at +∞, driven by the positive quadratic trend. Of course, in our model, this value is never reached in practice, because–for increasing values of the membrane potential–the negative parabola brings the membrane potential back with increasing speed. In this approximation, the firing rate definition reduces to (see Limitations of the model section)
(25)
r(t,η)=limVth→∞1πx(t,η)[Vth−y(t,η)]2+x2(t,η)⋅[(R−(Vth−c−)2+I−)+Iext+Js(Esyn−Vth)]=R−πx(t,η). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{align*} r(t, \eta)= & \lim _{V_{t h} \rightarrow \infty} \frac{1}{\pi} \frac{x(t, \eta)}{\left[V_{t h}-y(t, \eta)\right]^{2}+x^{2}(t, \eta)} \\ & \cdot\left[\left(R_{-}\left(V_{t h}-c_{-}\right)^{2}+I_{-}\right)+I_{\mathrm{ext}}+J s\left(E_{s y n}-V_{t h}\right)\right] \tag{25}\\ = & \frac{R_{-}}{\pi} x(t, \eta) . \end{align*}$$\end{document}

Following [59] we approximate the time derivative
(27)
⟨n˙⟩=∫∂η∫∂V∫∂nn∂∂tρ(t,V,n,η)dndVdη=−∫∂η∫∂V∫∂nn(∂JV∂V+∂Jn∂n)dndVdη. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{align*} \langle\dot{n}\rangle & =\int_{\partial \eta} \int_{\partial V} \int_{\partial n} n \frac{\partial}{\partial t} \rho(t, V, n, \eta) \mathrm{d} n \mathrm{~d} V \mathrm{~d} \eta \\ & =-\int_{\partial \eta} \int_{\partial V} \int_{\partial n} n\left(\frac{\partial \mathcal{J}^{V}}{\partial V}+\frac{\partial \mathcal{J}^{n}}{\partial n}\right) \mathrm{d} n \mathrm{~d} V \mathrm{~d} \eta . \tag{27} \end{align*}$$\end{document}
The first term is integrated by parts, yielding
(28)
Term1=∫∂η∫∂nn(∫−∞V⋆∂J−V∂VdV+∫V⋆Vmax∂J+V∂VdV)dndη=∫∂η∫∂nn(∫−∞V⋆dJ−V(t,V,n,s,η)+∫V⋆VmaxdJ+V(t,V,n,s,η))dndη=∫∂η∫∂nn(J−V|V⋆−J−V|−∞+J+V|Vmax−J+V|V⋆)dndη=0 \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{align*} \operatorname{Term} 1 & =\int_{\partial \eta} \int_{\partial n} n\left(\int_{-\infty}^{V^{\star}} \frac{\partial \mathcal{J}_{-}^{V}}{\partial V} \mathrm{~d} V+\int_{V^{\star}}^{V_{\max }} \frac{\partial \mathcal{J}_{+}^{V}}{\partial V} \mathrm{~d} V\right) \mathrm{d} n \mathrm{~d} \eta \\ & =\int_{\partial \eta} \int_{\partial n} n\left(\int_{-\infty}^{V^{\star}} \mathrm{d} \mathcal{J}_{-}^{V}(t, V, n, s, \eta)+\int_{V^{\star}}^{V_{\max }} \mathrm{d} \mathcal{J}_{+}^{V}(t, V, n, s, \eta)\right) \mathrm{d} n \mathrm{~d} \eta \\ & =\int_{\partial \eta} \int_{\partial n} n\left(\left.\mathcal{J}_{-}^{V}\right|_{V^{\star}}-\left.\mathcal{J}_{-}^{V}\right|_{-\infty}+\left.\mathcal{J}_{+}^{V}\right|_{V_{\max }}-\left.\mathcal{J}_{+}^{V}\right|_{V^{\star}}\right) \mathrm{d} n \mathrm{~d} \eta \\ & =0 \tag{28} \end{align*}$$\end{document}
Where JV\begin{document}$\mathcal{J}^{V}$\end{document} and J+V\begin{document}$\mathcal{J}_{+}^{V}$\end{document} indicate the flux for V ≤ V ⋆ and V ≥ V ⋆ (preserved at V⋆, and governed by the positive and negative parabolas (21)), respectively, and where we assumed that the flux at V→ − ∞ and V →Vmax is zero (a safe assumption, as a neuron is pushed back toward V ⋆ with (quadratic) infinite speed at these extreme values). The second term gives
(29)
Term2=∫∂η∫∂V∫∂nn∂Jn∂ndndVdη=∫∂η∫∂V∫∂nndJndVdη=∫∂η∫∂V(nJn|∂n−∫∂nJndn)dVdη=−∫∂η∫∂V∫∂nJndndVdη=−∫∂η∫∂V∫∂nGn(⋅)ρ(t,V,n,η)dndVdη=−⟨Gn(t,V,n)⟩ \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{align*} \text { Term } 2 & =\int_{\partial \eta} \int_{\partial V} \int_{\partial n} n \frac{\partial \mathcal{J}^{n}}{\partial n} \mathrm{~d} n \mathrm{~d} V \mathrm{~d} \eta \\ & =\int_{\partial \eta} \int_{\partial V} \int_{\partial n} n \mathrm{~d} \mathcal{J}^{n} \mathrm{~d} V \mathrm{~d} \eta \\ & =\int_{\partial \eta} \int_{\partial V}\left(\left.n \mathcal{J}^{n}\right|_{\partial n}-\int_{\partial n} \mathcal{J}^{n} \mathrm{~d} n\right) \mathrm{d} V \mathrm{~d} \eta \tag{29}\\ & =-\int_{\partial \eta} \int_{\partial V} \int_{\partial n} \mathcal{J}^{n} \mathrm{~d} n \mathrm{~d} V \mathrm{~d} \eta \\ & =-\int_{\partial \eta} \int_{\partial V} \int_{\partial n} G^{n}(\cdot) \rho(t, V, n, \eta) \mathrm{d} n \mathrm{~d} V \mathrm{~d} \eta \\ & =-\left\langle G^{n}(t, V, n)\right\rangle \end{align*}$$\end{document}
Imposing ⟨n∞(V) ⟩= n∞(⟨ V ⟩), we obtain the approximated result (see Limitations of the model section)
(30)
⟨n˙⟩=Gn(t,⟨V⟩,⟨n⟩) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \langle\dot{n}\rangle=G^{n}(t,\langle V\rangle,\langle n\rangle) \tag{30} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

Substituting equations (31) and (23) in the continuity equation (20) we obtain a polynomial condition of the form
(33)
k3V3+k2V2+k1V+k0=0, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} k_{3} V^{3}+k_{2} V^{2}+k_{1} V+k_{0}=0, \tag{33} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
Since this condition must be satisfied for all values of V, the solution for x(η, t) and y(η, t) is obtained by imposing kI ≡ 0, ∀I = 0, 1, 2, 3, leading to the mean-field equations
(34)
x˙=2Axy+Bx,y˙=Ay2+By+C−Ax2. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{align*} & \dot{x}=2 A x y+B x, \\ & \dot{y}=A y^{2}+B y+C-A x^{2} . \tag{34} \end{align*}$$\end{document}
Defining ω(η, t) = x(η, t) + iy(η, t), we can recast the above equations in the complex form
(35)
ω˙=i(C−Aω2−iBω). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \dot{\omega}=i\left(C-A \omega^{2}-i B \omega\right) . \tag{35} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
The mean-field equations are derived by integrating out η from the equation above. According to (26), we find ω(η¯−iΔ,t)=x(t)+iy(t)\begin{document}$\omega(\bar{\eta}-i \Delta, t)=x(t)+i y(t)$\end{document}, that substituted in (35) gives
(36)
x˙={Δ+2R−(y−c−)x−Jrx;∀y≤V⋆Δ+2R+(y−c+)x−Jrx;∀y>V⋆,y˙={R−(y−c−)2+I−−R−x2+Jr(E−y)+η¯;∀y≤V⋆R+(y−c+)2+I+−R+x2+Jr(E−y)+η¯;∀y>V⋆, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{align*} & \dot{x}=\left\{\begin{array}{l} \Delta+2 R_{-}\left(y-c_{-}\right) x-\operatorname{Jr} x ; \quad \forall y \leq V^{\star} \\ \Delta+2 R_{+}\left(y-c_{+}\right) x-\operatorname{Jr} x ; \quad \forall y>V^{\star}, \end{array}\right. \tag{36}\\ & \dot{y}=\left\{\begin{array}{l} R_{-}\left(y-c_{-}\right)^{2}+I_{-}-R_{-} x^{2}+\operatorname{Jr}(E-y)+\bar{\eta} ; \quad \forall y \leq V^{\star} \\ R_{+}\left(y-c_{+}\right)^{2}+I_{+}-R_{+} x^{2}+\operatorname{Jr}(E-y)+\bar{\eta} ; \quad \forall y>V^{\star}, \end{array}\right. \end{align*}$$\end{document}
